 "drivingQuestion": "From where is it derived that a departing judge may not say he acquitted but was outvoted?",
 "outcome": "not_dialectical",
 "outcomeNote": "Baraita supplies the mishna's verses.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.31a.10",
   "daf": "31a",
   "sentenceId": 9,
   "he": "וּמִנַּיִין לִכְשֶׁיֵּצֵא כּוּ׳. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן, מִנַּיִין לִכְשֶׁיֵּצֵא לֹא יֹאמַר: הֲרֵינִי מְזַכֶּה וַחֲבֵירַי מְחַיְּיבִין, אֲבָל מָה אֶעֱשֶׂה שֶׁחֲבֵירַי רַבּוּ עָלַי? תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר ״לֹא תֵלֵךְ רָכִיל בְּעַמֶּיךָ״, וְאוֹמֵר ״הוֹלֵךְ רָכִיל מְגַלֶּה סוֹד״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And from where» is it derived that «when» the judge «leaves» the courtroom, he should not say: I deemed you exempt and my colleagues deemed you liable, but what can I do, as my colleagues outnumbered me and consequently you were deemed liable? About this it is stated: “You shall not go as a talebearer among your people” (Leviticus 19:16), and it says: “One who goes about as a talebearer reveals secrets, but one who is of a faithful spirit conceals a matter” (Proverbs 11:13). «The Sages taught» in a baraita: «From where» is it derived that «when» the judge «leaves he should not say: I deemed» you «exempt and my colleagues deemed» you «liable, but what can I do, as my colleagues outnumbered me» and consequently you were deemed liable? «The verse states: “You shall not go as a talebearer among your people”» (Leviticus 19:16), «and it says: “One who goes about as a talebearer reveals secrets”» (Proverbs 11:13)."
  }
 ],
